  "text": "The rain hammered against the corrugated tin roof of the abandoned shack, a relentless rhythm that masked the silence within. I sat on the warped floorboards, my hands wrapped around a mug of cold coffee, watching the steam dissipate into the damp air. Outside, the forest was a wall of black shadows, indistinguishable from the night itself. I had come here to escape the noise of the city, to find clarity in the isolation, but the solitude felt less like peace and more like a cage. A sudden creak from the doorframe made me freeze. My breath hitched in my throat as I stared at the heavy oak door, which was slowly, inch by inch, sliding open. No wind blew, yet the hinges groaned with the weight of something entering. I reached for the flashlight on the table, my fingers clumsy with fear. The beam cut through the darkness, illuminating a figure standing in the doorway. It was not a person, but a shape composed of swirling mist and jagged shadows, its form shifting and unstable. It did not speak, but a voice echoed directly in my mind, cold and ancient, asking why I had disturbed its rest. I wanted to run, to scream, but my legs were leaden, rooted to the spot. The mist crept forward, touching the edge of my sleeve, and I felt a sudden, overwhelming cold that seeped into my bones. The flashlight flickered and died, plunging me into absolute darkness. In that void, I heard the whisper again, closer now, intimate and terrifying, promising that I would never leave this place, that I had become part of the story the forest had been telling for centuries. The door slammed shut behind me, sealing my fate in the dark."
